    "scenario": "BGP Backdoor",
    "goals": "• All IP addresses have been preconfigured for you.\n• Configure IBGP on router {{router3_name}} and {{router4_name}} using their Loopback0 interfaces.\n  AS {{as3}}: {{router3_name}}, {{router4_name}}\n• Configure EBGP between AS {{as1}} and AS {{as3}}.\n  AS {{as1}}: {{router1_name}}\n• Configure EBGP between AS {{as2}} and AS {{as3}}.\n  AS {{as2}}: {{router2_name}}\n• Router {{router1_name}}: advertise the {{router1_loopback0_network}} /{{router1_loopback0_prefix_len}} network in BGP.\n• Router {{router2_name}}: advertise the {{router2_loopback0_network}} /{{router2_loopback0_prefix_len}} network in BGP.\n• Ensure you can ping {{router2_loopback0_ip}} from router {{router1_name}}.\n• Ensure you can ping {{router1_loopback0_ip}} from router {{router2_name}}.\n• If you have any connectivity problems fix them by using next-hop-self or advertising the appropriate networks in BGP. Do not advertise the {{link1_network}} /{{link1_prefix_len}} network in BGP.\n• Configure EIGRP between router {{router1_name}} and {{router2_name}}:\n Advertise the {{link1_network}} /{{link1_prefix_len}} network.\n Advertise the {{router1_loopback0_network}} /{{router1_loopback0_prefix_len}} network.\n Advertise the {{router2_loopback0_network}} /{{router2_loopback0_prefix_len}} network.\n Disable auto-summary\n• Ensure you have an EIGRP neighbor relationship between the 2 routers.\n• Use a BGP backdoor command to make sure that traffic from router {{router2_name}} towards {{router1_loopback0_ip}} is using the {{link1_network}} /{{link1_prefix_len}} link and is not being sent through AS {{as3}}. When the link between router {{router1_name}} and {{router2_name}} fails it should use AS {{as3}} to reach the {{router1_loopback0_network}} /{{router1_loopback0_prefix_len}} network.\n• Use a BGP backdoor command to make sure that traffic from router {{router1_name}} towards {{router2_loopback0_ip}} is using the {{link1_network}} /{{link1_prefix_len}} link and is not being sent through AS {{as3}}. When the link between router {{router1_name}} and {{router2_name}} fails it should use AS {{as3}} to reach the {{router2_loopback0_network}} /{{router2_loopback0_prefix_len}} network.",
